#---
# purpose: "a rendered closure reports its arity, not its capture count -- fz-gk4"
#---
#
# # closure_render_arity
#
# Both closures capture exactly one value (`total`), so a renderer that prints
# the environment size cannot tell them apart. Their arities differ (0 and 2),
# which is what Elixir's `#Function<.../arity>` reports and what `#fn<id/n>`
# reports here.
#
# Paper answer.
#
# A closure body is a lifted function whose activation input row is its captures
# followed by its declared parameters, so an activation's input count equals its
# clause's parameter count by construction:
#
#   fn (x, acc) -> x + acc end      0 captures, arity 2, input row 2
#   nullary = fn () -> total end    1 capture (total), arity 0, input row 1
#   binary  = fn (x, y) -> ...      1 capture (total), arity 2, input row 3
#   main/0                          0 captures, arity 0, input row 0
#
# Arity is a static property of the function a closure was minted from, never an
# observed one. Both dbg calls here reach one callee activation of one input, so the
# callable surfaces seen of that slot are the union of everything that arrives at
# it. Projecting that union back onto an argument has to keep each member's own
# arity: an arity-0 surface belongs to nullary and says nothing about binary. A
# value that takes its surface from a different member of the union is minted with
# that member's input row, and its own clause then indexes past the end of it.
#
def main() do
  xs = [1, 2, 3]
  total = Enum.reduce(xs, 0, fn (x, acc) -> x + acc end)

  nullary = fn () -> total end
  binary = fn (x, y) -> x + y + total end

  dbg(nullary)
  dbg(binary)
end
